1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,600
For the last few years, the standard

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:08,400
approach to AI has been straightforward.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,960
Build one massive model and throw every

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:13,120
problem at it. If you need code written,

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:15,759
a database searched, or a math problem

6
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:18,800
solved, you send it to a generalist like

7
00:00:15,759 --> 00:00:21,600
GPT5 and expect it to process every

8
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:24,000
single task in the workflow. Relying on

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:26,560
a single massive system for everything

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,880
is incredibly expensive. Running

11
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,960
hundreds of billions of parameters just

12
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:33,120
to execute a simple web search or

13
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:36,239
trigger an API burns through compute

14
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:38,480
budgets. Worse, asking a language model

15
00:00:36,239 --> 00:00:40,800
to handle strict logic tasks without

16
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:43,360
external calculators or interpreters

17
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,040
routinely leads to hallucinations.

18
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,960
You might think the solution is to

19
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,440
prompt that large model to act as a

20
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:51,920
manager, telling it to hand off tasks to

21
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:54,160
smaller specialized tools. But when

22
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:56,559
researchers tested this, they found a

23
00:00:54,160 --> 00:01:00,399
persistent flaw called self-enhancement

24
00:00:56,559 --> 00:01:03,039
bias. If you ask GPT5 to route tasks

25
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:05,360
efficiently, it overwhelmingly ignores

26
00:01:03,039 --> 00:01:07,680
third party tools and defaults to its

27
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:09,840
own mini version, regardless of whether

28
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:12,880
it's the best or cheapest option for the

29
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:15,119
job. Prompting a massive AI to manage

30
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,920
tools triggers this self-enhancement

31
00:01:15,119 --> 00:01:20,400
bias. Because the model favors its own

32
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:22,880
developmental variance over actual cost

33
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,640
or utility, you cannot rely on an

34
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:27,439
off-the-shelf system to act as a

35
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:29,600
manager. True orchestration requires a

36
00:01:27,439 --> 00:01:31,520
dedicated purpose-built routing model

37
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:34,640
that is specifically trained to weigh

38
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:37,119
the exact cost, latency, and accuracy of

39
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,880
every available tool. This requires

40
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:40,960
moving away from the single brain

41
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:42,880
approach. We are shifting toward a

42
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:44,720
corporate style structure where a

43
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,960
specialized lightweight orchestrator

44
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:49,119
model sits at the center dynamically

45
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,360
delegating tasks to a vast array of

46
00:01:49,119 --> 00:01:53,280
specialized workers. Training that

47
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:55,280
dedicated orchestrator requires an

48
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:57,520
enormous amount of data showing perfect

49
00:01:55,280 --> 00:02:00,159
tool use. This creates a severe

50
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:02,000
bottleneck. While we have pabytes of

51
00:02:00,159 --> 00:02:04,479
human text on the internet to train

52
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,479
language models, we have almost zero

53
00:02:04,479 --> 00:02:08,959
recorded logs of the optimal thought

54
00:02:06,479 --> 00:02:11,599
process and API sequences humans use

55
00:02:08,959 --> 00:02:13,760
when solving complex problems. Because

56
00:02:11,599 --> 00:02:15,920
human tool use data doesn't exist at

57
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:18,560
scale, researchers have to manufacture

58
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:20,400
it. They build virtual environments that

59
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,239
generate their own training data through

60
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:24,800
a closed loop generation and

61
00:02:22,239 --> 00:02:27,680
verification process. Starting with a

62
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:30,000
broad domain, an AI generates a complete

63
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:32,480
virtual database schema, populates it

64
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,319
with thousands of fake consistent rows,

65
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,800
and writes the Python functions needed

66
00:02:34,319 --> 00:02:38,959
to query it. Once the virtual world

67
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:41,599
exists, a teacher model looks at the

68
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:43,680
database and asks a specific question.

69
00:02:41,599 --> 00:02:45,920
Because the system created the database,

70
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:48,319
it can execute the query itself to find

71
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:50,160
the exact answer. This automatic

72
00:02:48,319 --> 00:02:52,480
verification creates a perfect

73
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:54,560
groundtruth label, linking the original

74
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:56,959
question to the precise sequence of tool

75
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:59,360
calls needed to solve it. To ensure

76
00:02:56,959 --> 00:03:01,280
quality, the system generates eight

77
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:04,319
