78
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:06,879
problem. If none work, it is discarded.

79
00:03:04,319 --> 00:03:09,519
If multiple paths succeed, the system

80
00:03:06,879 --> 00:03:12,239
filters the results using a pass at 8

81
00:03:09,519 --> 00:03:14,480
criteria, keeping only the single most

82
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:17,200
efficient successful trajectory to use

83
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:19,599
as a positive reinforcement sample.

84
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,680
Manufacturing verifiable data this way

85
00:03:19,599 --> 00:03:24,640
solves the volume problem, but it

86
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:26,879
introduces synthetic bias. The resulting

87
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:29,360
orchestrator becomes highly efficient at

88
00:03:26,879 --> 00:03:31,440
executing paths, but its core reasoning

89
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:33,200
capabilities are permanently capped by

90
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:35,040
the intelligence of the teacher model

91
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:37,760
used to generate the environment in the

92
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:40,000
first place. Once the synthetic data is

93
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:42,239
manufactured, the focus shifts to the

94
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,159
actual training. Researchers use

95
00:03:42,239 --> 00:03:46,400
reinforcement learning to teach an 8

96
00:03:44,159 --> 00:03:48,959
billion parameter model exactly how to

97
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:50,560
sequence those tool calls. This chart

98
00:03:48,959 --> 00:03:52,959
shows the loss landscape during

99
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:54,720
reinforcement learning. It highlights a

100
00:03:52,959 --> 00:03:56,879
massive hurdle called the variance

101
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:59,519
problem. Because the gradient estimates

102
00:03:56,879 --> 00:04:01,519
are noisy, a single lucky roll out where

103
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:03,680
the model accidentally guesses the right

104
00:04:01,519 --> 00:04:06,159
sequence can trigger a massive weight

105
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:08,400
update. That sudden shift causes

106
00:04:06,159 --> 00:04:10,560
catastrophic forgetting where the model

107
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:13,280
breaks its own language circuits just to

108
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:15,280
maximize a reward. To stabilize this

109
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:17,759
erratic training process, you have to

110
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:19,440
run massive batch sizes. Training a

111
00:04:17,759 --> 00:04:22,639
reinforcement learning orchestrator

112
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:25,600
requires a dedicated cluster of 16 H100

113
00:04:22,639 --> 00:04:27,440
GPUs. Not to speed up the process as a

114
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:29,520
strict mathematical necessity for

115
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:31,759
variance reduction. By averaging

116
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,600
hundreds of trajectories simultaneously,

117
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:35,759
the system filters out the noise and

118
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:38,000
finds a stable, reliable direction for

119
00:04:35,759 --> 00:04:40,240
the model to improve. Because these

120
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,120
trajectories vary wildly in length from

121
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:45,360
a 500 token web search to a 12,000 token

122
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:47,600
debugging loop, engineers use sequence

123
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:49,680
packing, they concatenate multiple short

124
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:52,080
sequences into a single long context

125
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:54,000
window, ensuring the GPUs never waste

126
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:56,880
compute cycles processing empty padding

127
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,199
tokens. This entire infrastructure setup

128
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:01,759
is designed to solve a stability versus

129
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:03,919
plasticity dilemma. The model has to be

130
00:05:01,759 --> 00:05:06,240
plastic enough to learn highly specific

131
00:05:03,919 --> 00:05:08,000
routing and tool skills, but stable

132
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:10,160
enough that it maintains its baseline

133
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
language comprehension. Once the

134
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:14,960
orchestrator is trained, it has to be

135
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,960
evaluated. But as Goodart's law states,

136
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:19,280
when a measure becomes a target, it

137
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,680
ceases to be a good measure. Standard

138
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:23,680
benchmarks like MMLU are heavily

139
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,759
saturated and effectively part of the

140
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:27,840
training data, meaning they tell us very

141
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:30,160
little about a model's actual ability to

142
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:32,080
